#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
homedepot_ca_checker.py
=======================

Home Depot CANADA companion to omsid_checker.py.

Reads an Excel file containing Home Depot Canada product IDs, matches them
against Home Depot Canada's published XML sitemaps, and writes an Excel
workbook giving the live product URL or "N/A" for each item.

WHY SITEMAPS
------------
Home Depot's interactive product pages sit behind Akamai Bot Manager and
return 403 to automated clients. Their XML sitemaps do not: they exist so
Google and Bing can crawl the catalogue, so they are served openly. Every
Home Depot Canada product URL ends in the product ID, which means the sitemap
files already contain both halves of what we need. Matching is then a local
VLOOKUP against a large table rather than hundreds of blocked HTTP requests.

CANADIAN URL SHAPES (verified)
------------------------------
    English : https://www.homedepot.ca/product/<slug>/<PRODUCT_ID>
    French  : https://www.homedepot.ca/produit/<slug>/<PRODUCT_ID>

CANADIAN SITEMAP FILES (verified)
---------------------------------
    https://www.homedepot.ca/sitemap_product_en.1.xml
    https://www.homedepot.ca/sitemap_product_en.2.xml   ... and so on
    https://www.homedepot.ca/sitemap_product_fr.1.xml   ... and so on

These are flat, numbered files rather than the nested index tree used by
homedepot.com. The script still tries robots.txt and the usual index paths
first, and only falls back to enumerating the numbered files.

IMPORTANT: CANADIAN IDS ARE NOT US OMSIDS
-----------------------------------------
A US OMSID (Internet #) is normally 9 digits, for example 206856244.
A Home Depot Canada product ID is normally 10 digits beginning with 100,
for example 1001015306. They are separate catalogues with separate numbering.
Feeding US OMSIDs into this script will return NOT_FOUND for nearly every
row. Set MODE = "diagnose" if you want the script to tell you which kind of
IDs your input file actually contains before you run the full crawl.

HOW TO RUN (PyCharm, no terminal needed)
----------------------------------------
    1. One time only, install the three dependencies. In PyCharm:
       File > Settings > Project > Python Interpreter > "+" and add
       pandas, openpyxl, requests.
    2. Put your Excel file in the same folder as this script.
    3. Edit INPUT_FILE below to match its name.
    4. Leave MODE = "sitemap_test" and press Run. This confirms the
       Canadian sitemaps are reachable from your machine. It takes well
       under a minute.
    5. If that succeeds, set MODE = "sitemap" and press Run again.

MODES
-----
    "sitemap_test"  Connectivity check. Confirms the sitemaps respond and
                    that product IDs parse out of the URLs. Downloads little.
    "diagnose"      Reads your Excel file only. Reports the detected ID
                    column, how the IDs are shaped, and whether they look
                    Canadian or American. No network access at all.
    "sitemap"       The real run. Builds the ID-to-URL index, matches your
                    file, writes the output workbook.
    "audit"         Same as "sitemap" but also prints every unmatched ID to
                    the console for eyeballing.
    "spotcheck"     Takes a few resolved URLs and requests them directly, to
                    see whether product pages respond from your machine.
                    Informational only. A 403 here does NOT mean the URL is
                    wrong; it means Akamai blocked the request.

Author's note: NOT_FOUND means "absent from Home Depot Canada's published
sitemaps." That is strong evidence an item is not live, but sitemaps
regenerate on a cycle, so a very recently listed item can be live on site and
still missing here. That is why unmatched rows land on their own tab.
"""

